   'Lower the loads to a weight you can lift for several smooth reps, such as your bodyweight for the squat and deadlift and half your bodyweight for the press. Newer athletes can use a fixed bar or dumbbells and keep the same ladder. Work the ladder for 15 minutes and score total reps the same way.',
   '[{"question": "What is the Otis workout?", "answer": "As many reps as possible in 15 minutes of a ladder: 1 back squat, 1 shoulder press and 1 deadlift, then 2 of each, then 3 of each, and so on. Squats and deadlifts use 1½ times your body weight, presses use ¾ of your body weight."}, {"question": "How do I count my score?", "answer": "Count every rep of every lift. Each finished rung adds three times its number, so after the rung of 5 you have 45 reps and after the rung of 10 you have 165."}, {"question": "Who was Otis?", "answer": "U.S. Marine Lt. Col. Christopher \"Otis\" Raible, 40, of North Huntington, Pennsylvania, commanding officer of Marine Attack Squadron 211. He was killed during an attack on Camp Bastion, Afghanistan, on Sept. 14, 2012."}, {"question": "Do women use the same loads?", "answer": "Yes. CrossFit sets the loads by body weight, so everyone uses 1½ times body weight for squats and deadlifts and ¾ for the press."}]'::jsonb,
